    "see": "../PROVENANCE.md"
  },
  "what_this_is": "For a handful of (z,x,y) in each golden, the payload length and sha256 that go-pmtiles returns. The payloads themselves are not committed twice: the archives hold them.",
  "how_each_number_was_obtained": {
    "length / sha256": "`pmtiles tile --quiet <archive> <z> <x> <y>` redirected to its own file, then the file hashed. No pipe carries the exit status.",
    "exit_code": "the CLI's exit status for that invocation",
    "directory_entry_length / offset": "the EntryV3 that covers this (z,x,y), from pmtiles.DeserializeEntries over the same archive",
    "directly_addressed_by_an_entry": "false means the tile is served by a run, so the entry that covers it carries a lower tile_id",
    "length_matches_entry": "a cross-check of the two independent paths, computed here"
  },
  "tile_compression_note": "every golden has tile_compression = 1 (none), so what `pmtiles tile` writes is the stored payload byte for byte, with no decompression step in between",
  "absent_behaviour": "a request for an in-range tile the archive does not hold exits 0 and writes zero bytes. It is not an error to go-pmtiles, so an empty result means 'absent', not 'failed'. These rows are the negative control for the hit list. See FINDINGS.md.",
  "out_of_range_behaviour": "these rows have x or y past 2**z - 1. go-pmtiles does NOT refuse them: ZxyToID wraps the coordinate into range and the CLI serves whatever tile it lands on, with exit code 0 and a full payload. Our reader has to refuse these, not reproduce them, so they are recorded as behaviour and not as vectors. See FINDINGS.md.",
  "length_mismatches_found": 0,
